WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.730 --> 00:00:09.500
Good afternoon everyone, my name is Ben Busby
and I am the genomics outreach coordinator

00:00:09.500 --> 00:00:17.980
for NCBI. In the next 5 to 7 minutes I will
show you four different ways to get data from

00:00:17.980 --> 00:00:20.380
the Genome in a Bottle project.

00:00:20.380 --> 00:00:27.900
With me, virutally, I have the head of the
education group, Peter Cooper and Justin Zook,

00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:35.770
a primary investigator at NIST who really
spearheads the Genome in a Bottle project.

00:00:35.770 --> 00:00:43.789
This consortium is a collaboration between
NIST, FDA, NCBI and a whole bunch of other

00:00:43.789 --> 00:00:53.969
people including volunteers to make data on
which one can benchmark bioinformatics and

00:00:53.969 --> 00:01:01.289
sequencing methodologies and look for regions
with questionable variant calls and also to

00:01:01.289 --> 00:01:05.400
identify high confidence variants.

00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:11.650
In the next 5 to 7 minutes I will show you
four ways to get the data and if you have

00:01:11.650 --> 00:01:17.320
any questions please type them into the question
pod.

00:01:17.320 --> 00:01:26.200
One very quick way to get the data at NCBI
is to go to the Genome in a Bottle BioProjects.

00:01:26.200 --> 00:01:35.380
If we were to expand this out we would see
a large abstract of what this is, and also

00:01:35.380 --> 00:01:42.680
there is a link to the dedicated FTP site
which I will show you at the end that has

00:01:42.680 --> 00:01:51.080
the cutting edge Genome in a Bottle data as
well as variant calls done by NIST and some

00:01:51.080 --> 00:01:52.970
other groups.

00:01:52.970 --> 00:02:01.040
The Genome in a Bottle data that is in BioProject
and SRA currently has 13 BioSamples and when

00:02:01.040 --> 00:02:07.230
you look at these here you see that some are
from the 1000 genomes project and there are

00:02:07.230 --> 00:02:13.349
other trios from other projects such as the
PGP.

00:02:13.349 --> 00:02:19.870
Also you can look at the SRA experiment associated
with the Genome in a Bottle project. You can

00:02:19.870 --> 00:02:31.209
see a list here. They are filterable using
Entrez filtering, and when we filter we can

00:02:31.209 --> 00:02:40.900
look at the aligned datasets such as the Complete
Genomics dataset for the Ashkenazim Son. There

00:02:40.900 --> 00:02:45.969
will be more aligned datasets coming into
the SRA in the next few weeks.

00:02:45.969 --> 00:02:52.060
When we click on the actual SRA run we can
see the reference genome that it was aligned

00:02:52.060 --> 00:02:59.760
to, the coverage and some other information
about the alignment. If you are interested

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:13.430
in streaming aligned data, please check out
the webinar we did on Feb 235. Also, unaligned

00:03:13.430 --> 00:03:23.209
data and some aligned data can be investigated
using SRA blast. We did a webinar on that

00:03:23.209 --> 00:03:29.139
last November 5 and please feel free to check
that out.

00:03:29.139 --> 00:03:35.799
The other thing we can do with all of this
data is investigate the metadata by sending

00:03:35.799 --> 00:03:44.349
these projects to the SRA run selector. This
run selector is SOLR-facetable resource where

00:03:44.349 --> 00:03:54.739
one can look at available datasets that are
in SRA for many BioSamples included in the

00:03:54.739 --> 00:04:02.010
Genome in a Bottle project. There are many
things that you can facet on. I've shown you

00:04:02.010 --> 00:04:09.370
just a few that are faceting by BioSample
and platform.

00:04:09.370 --> 00:04:18.389
Here we can see that we can filter by just
the Illumina and PacBio for one individual.

00:04:18.389 --> 00:04:26.440
We can grab an accession list from that filtering
and we can use this to loop over using the

00:04:26.440 --> 00:04:36.870
sra-toolkit if we want to stream data, particularly
parts of the genome or gene loci. These are

00:04:36.870 --> 00:04:42.190
very useful, in my opinion, for benchmarking
algorithms without downloading and mapping

00:04:42.190 --> 00:04:44.030
the entire genome.

00:04:44.030 --> 00:04:49.600
Another place one can get genotype calls from
some of the Genome in a Bottle data, although

00:04:49.600 --> 00:04:56.070
it is not necessarily a primary source of
the Genome in a Bottle datam is from the GetRM

00:04:56.070 --> 00:05:05.950
project. We have links here directly to some
of the vcf and bed files produced by the NIST

00:05:05.950 --> 00:05:13.670
group and there is also an associated FTP
site where we see variant calls from a bunch

00:05:13.670 --> 00:05:23.200
of different groups. The project also has
a browser in which we can look at specific

00:05:23.200 --> 00:05:26.370
genes and some of the variant calls.

00:05:26.370 --> 00:05:33.130
I would just like to show you in the next
20 or 30 seconds some features of this browser.

00:05:33.130 --> 00:05:40.090
You can see variant calls done by a number
of different groups as well as a genotype

00:05:40.090 --> 00:05:44.550
browser where you can see where the calls
agree.

00:05:44.550 --> 00:05:51.940
There is pretty good concordance between groups
for most genomic regions.

00:05:51.940 --> 00:05:59.570
The most authoritative place to get Genome
in a Bottle data is from the Genome in a Bottle

00:05:59.570 --> 00:06:09.070
FTP site. If you look at the README.ftp_structure
there is an updated outline of where you can

00:06:09.070 --> 00:06:15.840
get all of the data sets including recently
updated data.

00:06:15.840 --> 00:06:24.290
If you go a little bit farther into the data
you can see data broken out by specific BioSamples

00:06:24.290 --> 00:06:31.380
and by going into analysis one can go ahead
and get the genotype calls for some of these

00:06:31.380 --> 00:06:34.190
individuals.

00:06:34.190 --> 00:06:42.200
One can get the latest data by going into
ftp/release/*/latest/ and you can get calls

00:06:42.200 --> 00:06:47.150
as well as bed files from that location.

00:06:47.150 --> 00:06:52.430
If you'd like to get involved in this Genome
in a Bottle project, some ways you could help

00:06:52.430 --> 00:06:59.960
are by generating structural variants for any
of the Ashkenazim trio and compare and integrate

00:06:59.960 --> 00:07:07.840
those calls that you submit. You can compare
and integrate phasing and also give feedback

00:07:07.840 --> 00:07:12.880
as we see some of these high confidence calls.

00:07:12.880 --> 00:07:19.340
If you are interested in getting involved
you can contact me, BenBusby at nih.gov or

00:07:19.340 --> 00:07:22.530
Justin.Zook at nist.gov.

00:07:22.530 --> 00:07:28.160
With that what we would like to do is open
the floor up to questions.

00:07:28.160 --> 00:07:36.720
So, some people are having trouble accessing
the trace website. And I have been asked to

00:07:36.720 --> 00:07:48.780
restate it. The quickest way to find an accurate
URL is to go to NCBI BioProjects, search for

00:07:48.780 --> 00:07:58.660
Genome in a Bottle, find that BioProject and
expand the abstract. ftp-trace.ncbi.nih.gov/giab/ftp/

00:07:58.660 --> 00:08:05.030
Another question that I think is more appropriate
for Justin is, are there any plans to liftover

00:08:05.030 --> 00:08:12.260
to GRCh38?

00:08:12.260 --> 00:08:20.060
That's a good question and we have talked
a lot about this. Our plans are generally

00:08:20.060 --> 00:08:30.230
to make calls on GRCh37 (hg19) first. We are
working on methods to make calls on GRCh38

00:08:30.230 --> 00:08:43.810
as well. We hope to have initial snp and indel
calls in the next 4 to 6 months probably.

00:08:43.810 --> 00:08:50.939
Those will probably be without using the Alt
sequences initially, but we are interested

00:08:50.939 --> 00:08:54.800
in using methods for the Alts.

00:08:54.800 --> 00:09:00.930
Another person asks, what level of concordance
do you generally expect to see now when looking

00:09:00.930 --> 00:09:05.350
only at snps and short indels?

00:09:05.350 --> 00:09:11.920
This really depends a lot on exactly what
you are looking at. If you are looking in

00:09:11.920 --> 00:09:19.600
the easier regions of the genome you can really
see very close to 100% concordance as long

00:09:19.600 --> 00:09:27.759
as you have good coverage. When you start
getting to more difficult regions there is

00:09:27.759 --> 00:09:35.389
often high discordance. Numbers that I often
see if you take two different bioinformatics

00:09:35.389 --> 00:09:42.920
algorithms or two different sequencing technologies
then you have somewhere around 5 to 10% disagreement

00:09:42.920 --> 00:09:49.680
between the two methods over the whole genome.
If down select to the easier portions of the

00:09:49.680 --> 00:09:53.319
genome, you can really have close to 100%
concordance.

00:09:53.319 --> 00:09:59.009
A more straightforward and fun question, have
there been samples deposited from the X Ten

00:09:59.009 --> 00:10:00.430
Illumina platform?

00:10:00.430 --> 00:10:05.910
I don't think there have quite yet but I know
some people have been generating these and

00:10:05.910 --> 00:10:12.339
I believe there is an X Ten data set from
the Garvan Institute that is available for

00:10:12.339 --> 00:10:20.199
our pilot genome, NA12878. I think if you
search for them you will be able to find them.

00:10:20.199 --> 00:10:32.040
And just to make sure that they are not talking about the 10X platform, which is often confusing.

00:10:32.040 --> 00:10:39.339
I think they actually do mean the X Ten Illumina
platform. But the 10X data, 10

00:10:39.339 --> 00:10:46.269
X Genomics, recently released data from NA12878 and
the Ashkenazim trio. It's all available on

00:10:46.269 --> 00:10:47.879
the FTP site.

00:10:47.879 --> 00:10:53.399
All right, once again, if you have any other
questions about things that we have covered

00:10:53.399 --> 00:10:58.529
such as BioProjects or Alts, please free to
check out our archived webinars, subscribe

00:10:58.529 --> 00:11:08.110
to our YouTube channel or email me the questions
or email questions to webinars at ncbi.nlm.nih.gov.

00:11:08.110 --> 00:11:15.139
Thank you very much everybody and we will
see you soon for other NCBI webinars.

